## Memory Optimization: Register Blocking with Multi-Level Caching

### Objective
Maximize arithmetic intensity by combining SLM tiling with register-level blocking. Each work-item computes multiple output elements, amortizing memory access costs and keeping data in the fastest storage (registers).

### Required Transformations

**1. Implement Register Tiles for Per-Work-Item Accumulation**
Each work-item maintains a private register tile (not shared with other work-items):

```cpp
// Configuration: each work-item computes a THREAD_M × THREAD_N output block
constexpr int THREAD_M = 4;  // Output rows per work-item
constexpr int THREAD_N = 4;  // Output cols per work-item
constexpr int TILE_K = 16;   // K-dimension tile size in SLM

// Register tile for accumulation (private to each work-item)
float reg_C[THREAD_M][THREAD_N] = {{0.0f}};

// Iterate over K-dimension tiles
for (int k_tile = 0; k_tile < K; k_tile += TILE_K) {
    // Phase 1: Collaborative load of A and B tiles into SLM
    // Each work-item loads multiple elements to fill the tile
    for (int i = 0; i < elements_per_workitem; ++i) {
        smem_A[load_row][load_col] = A[...];
        smem_B[load_row][load_col] = B[...];
    }
    sycl::group_barrier(item.get_group());

    // Phase 2: Compute from SLM into register tile
    #pragma unroll  // Critical: keeps values in registers
    for (int k = 0; k < TILE_K; ++k) {
        // Load fragments from SLM to registers
        float a_frag[THREAD_M], b_frag[THREAD_N];

        #pragma unroll
        for (int m = 0; m < THREAD_M; ++m)
            a_frag[m] = smem_A[local_m * THREAD_M + m][k];

        #pragma unroll
        for (int n = 0; n < THREAD_N; ++n)
            b_frag[n] = smem_B[k][local_n * THREAD_N + n];

        // Outer product accumulation into register tile
        #pragma unroll
        for (int m = 0; m < THREAD_M; ++m)
            #pragma unroll
            for (int n = 0; n < THREAD_N; ++n)
                reg_C[m][n] += a_frag[m] * b_frag[n];
    }
    sycl::group_barrier(item.get_group());
}

// Phase 3: Write register tile to global memory
#pragma unroll
for (int m = 0; m < THREAD_M; ++m)
    #pragma unroll
    for (int n = 0; n < THREAD_N; ++n)
        C[(out_row + m) * N + (out_col + n)] = reg_C[m][n];
```

**2. Choose Register Tile Sizes Based on Hardware**
| THREAD_M × THREAD_N | Registers Used | Use Case |
|---------------------|----------------|----------|
| 4 × 4               | 16 floats      | Balanced, good for most kernels |
| 8 × 4               | 32 floats      | Higher throughput, more register pressure |
| 8 × 8               | 64 floats      | Maximum reuse, requires sufficient registers |

**3. Always Use `#pragma unroll`**
Unrolling inner loops is mandatory to keep intermediate values in registers rather than spilling to memory.

### Constraints
- Register pressure: too large a tile causes register spilling (performance cliff)
- Ensure work-group size × THREAD_M × THREAD_N covers the output tile
- Handle boundary conditions when output dimensions aren't multiples of tile sizes

