## Parallelism Optimization: Sub-Group (SIMD) Collective Operations

### Objective
Leverage sub-group (SIMD lane) collective operations for lock-step parallelism without explicit synchronization. Sub-group operations execute on hardware SIMD units and are significantly faster than local memory-based approaches.

### Required Transformations

**1. Sub-Group Reduction (Preferred Over Tree Reduction)**
Replace manual tree reduction with built-in sub-group collectives:

```cpp
sycl::sub_group sg = item.get_sub_group();

// Single call replaces entire tree reduction within sub-group
float sg_sum = sycl::reduce_over_group(sg, my_value, sycl::plus<float>());
float sg_max = sycl::reduce_over_group(sg, my_value, sycl::maximum<float>());
float sg_min = sycl::reduce_over_group(sg, my_value, sycl::minimum<float>());
```

**2. Two-Level Reduction (Sub-Groups + Work-Group)**
For work-group-wide reduction, combine sub-group results:

```cpp
sycl::sub_group sg = item.get_sub_group();
int sg_id = sg.get_group_id()[0];       // Which sub-group within work-group
int sg_lid = sg.get_local_id()[0];      // Lane ID within sub-group
int num_subgroups = sg.get_group_range()[0];

// Level 1: Sub-group reduction (no barrier needed)
float sg_sum = sycl::reduce_over_group(sg, my_value, sycl::plus<float>());

// Store sub-group results for cross-sub-group reduction
auto sg_results = sycl::ext::oneapi::group_local_memory_for_overwrite<float[16]>(item.get_group());
if (sg_lid == 0) {
    sg_results[sg_id] = sg_sum;  // Lane 0 of each sub-group writes
}
sycl::group_barrier(item.get_group());

// Level 2: First sub-group reduces all sub-group results
float final_result = 0.0f;
if (sg_id == 0) {
    float val = (sg_lid < num_subgroups) ? sg_results[sg_lid] : 0.0f;
    final_result = sycl::reduce_over_group(sg, val, sycl::plus<float>());
}

// Broadcast result to all work-items if needed
final_result = sycl::group_broadcast(item.get_group(), final_result, 0);
```

**3. Sub-Group Communication Primitives**

| Operation | Usage | Example |
|-----------|-------|--------|
| `reduce_over_group` | Reduce all lanes to single value | `sum = reduce_over_group(sg, val, plus<>())` |
| `group_broadcast` | Copy one lane's value to all | `all_get = group_broadcast(sg, val, 0)` |
| `shift_group_left` | Get neighbor's value (left) | `right_nbr = shift_group_left(sg, val, 1)` |
| `shift_group_right` | Get neighbor's value (right) | `left_nbr = shift_group_right(sg, val, 1)` |
| `permute_group_by_xor` | XOR-based shuffle | `pair = permute_group_by_xor(sg, val, 1)` |

```cpp
// Example: Stencil operation using neighbor shuffles
sycl::sub_group sg = item.get_sub_group();
float left = sycl::shift_group_right(sg, my_value, 1);
float right = sycl::shift_group_left(sg, my_value, 1);
float stencil_result = 0.25f * left + 0.5f * my_value + 0.25f * right;
```

### Constraints
- Sub-group size is hardware-dependent (typically 8, 16, 32, or 64)
- Query with `sg.get_local_range()[0]` or `sg.get_max_local_range()[0]`
- Sub-group operations are implicit barriers within the sub-group only
- Cross-sub-group communication still requires local memory + barrier
- **CRITICAL**: `reduce_over_group` requires predefined SYCL function objects (`sycl::plus<T>()`, `sycl::maximum<T>()`, `sycl::minimum<T>()`, `sycl::multiplies<T>()`). Custom lambdas like `[](float a, float b) { return a > b ? a : b; }` cause compile errors!

