## Parallelism Optimization: Hierarchical Multi-Level Decomposition

### Objective
Structure computation at three distinct levels - work-group, sub-group, and work-item - to maximize data reuse at each level and minimize synchronization overhead.

### Required Transformations

**1. Three-Level Index Decomposition**
Map the output space to a hierarchy of tiles:

```cpp
// Configuration constants
constexpr int TILE_M = 128;       // Work-group output tile rows
constexpr int TILE_N = 128;       // Work-group output tile cols
constexpr int SUBTILE_M = 32;     // Sub-group tile rows
constexpr int SUBTILE_N = 64;     // Sub-group tile cols
constexpr int THREAD_M = 4;       // Per-work-item rows
constexpr int THREAD_N = 4;       // Per-work-item cols

// Level 1: Work-group - which output tile
int wg_row = item.get_group(0) * TILE_M;
int wg_col = item.get_group(1) * TILE_N;

// Level 2: Sub-group - which sub-tile within work-group tile
sycl::sub_group sg = item.get_sub_group();
int sg_id = sg.get_group_id()[0];
int num_sg_cols = TILE_N / SUBTILE_N;  // e.g., 128/64 = 2
int sg_row = (sg_id / num_sg_cols) * SUBTILE_M;
int sg_col = (sg_id % num_sg_cols) * SUBTILE_N;

// Level 3: Work-item (lane) - which elements within sub-tile
int lane = sg.get_local_id()[0];
int lane_cols = SUBTILE_N / THREAD_N;  // e.g., 64/4 = 16
int thread_row = (lane / lane_cols) * THREAD_M;
int thread_col = (lane % lane_cols) * THREAD_N;

// Final output position for this work-item
int out_row = wg_row + sg_row + thread_row;
int out_col = wg_col + sg_col + thread_col;
```

**2. Hierarchical Data Movement**

| Level | Storage | Scope | Movement |
|-------|---------|-------|----------|
| Work-group | SLM | All work-items in group | Collaborative load from global |
| Sub-group | Shuffles | All lanes in sub-group | Shuffle instead of SLM |
| Work-item | Registers | Private | Direct access |

```cpp
// Work-group level: collaborative load to SLM
for (int i = tid; i < TILE_ELEMENTS; i += WG_SIZE) {
    smem[i] = global_data[tile_offset + i];
}
sycl::group_barrier(item.get_group());

// Sub-group level: share data via shuffles (no SLM needed)
float my_a = smem[sg_row + lane];
for (int src_lane = 0; src_lane < SG_SIZE; ++src_lane) {
    float broadcasted_a = sycl::group_broadcast(sg, my_a, src_lane);
    // Use broadcasted_a for computation
}

// Work-item level: register tile accumulation
float reg_C[THREAD_M][THREAD_N] = {{0.0f}};
// ... accumulate into registers ...
```

**3. Load Coordination Example**
Each level is responsible for loading data for its scope:

```cpp
// Work-group loads entire A and B tiles to SLM (all work-items participate)
for (int idx = tid; idx < TILE_K * TILE_M; idx += WG_SIZE) {
    int row = idx / TILE_K, col = idx % TILE_K;
    smem_A[row][col] = A[(wg_row + row) * K + col];
}
// Similar for B tile
sycl::group_barrier(item.get_group());

// Sub-groups process sub-tiles, work-items accumulate register tiles
// Use shuffles within sub-group to avoid additional SLM traffic
```

### Constraints
- Tile sizes must divide evenly: TILE_M divisible by SUBTILE_M, SUBTILE_M by THREAD_M
- Sub-group shuffle requires all lanes to participate (no early exit)
- Total SLM usage must fit hardware limit (typically 64KB)
- Register tile size limited by register file (avoid spilling)

